        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.107,
            0.506,
            0.129
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Look at triangle ADM in Fig. 1.6."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.204,
            0.136,
            0.796,
            0.509
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 3,
              "bbox": [
                0.204,
                0.136,
                0.796,
                0.509
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_8_image_3.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.543,
            0.888,
            0.584
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Triangle ADM is an acute angled triangle in the first quadrant. How do we find the lengths of its sides AD, DM and MA?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.608,
            0.404,
            0.632
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think and Reflect"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.646,
            0.861,
            0.686
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. In moving from A (3, 4) to D (7, 1), what distance has been covered along the x-axis? What about the distance along the y-axis?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.688,
            0.689,
            0.708
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Can these distances help you find the distance AD?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.736,
            0.887,
            0.759
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fig. 1.7 gives us a clue. The distance moved along the x-axis is given by CD."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.765,
            0.808,
            0.786
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "CD = x-coordinate of D – x-coordinate of A = 7 – 3 = 4."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.794,
            0.713,
            0.816
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The distance moved along the y-axis is given by AC."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.822,
            0.855,
            0.867
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "AC = y-coordinate of A – y-coordinate of D = 4 – 1 = 3. Using the Baudhāyana–Pythagoras Theorem, we get the distance"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.363,
            0.874,
            0.634,
            0.899
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_8_equation_12.jpg",
          "content": "\\mathrm{AD} = \\sqrt{4^{2} + 3^{2}} = 5   units ."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
